    raiseFailure["domain/raiseFailure<br/>DEF-ROC-049 NEW - the ONE decision 'was the ticket created or not', and the ONE derivation of a failure's structured category (moved out of functions/soakSweep so a log line and the release decision cannot disagree, EXP-047). classifyCreateFailure: an ENUMERATED refused category (internal-bad-request/auth/forbidden/not-found = a 4xx or the adapter pre-flight guard) is ticket-not-created so the claim may be released and the next tick genuinely retries; EVERYTHING ELSE - 5xx-after-bounded-retries, network, and any unrecognised shape - is ticket-outcome-unknown, so the claim is KEPT because releasing it could raise a SECOND real PPSM Alert for one fault. FAIL-SAFE, no permissive fallback (v124): only the enumerated set releases. The categories are the REAL ones JiraApiAdapter throws, pinned by driving the REAL adapter over a scripted transport (AC-DEF-ROC-049.6) - a NAMED control is not a VERIFIED control (DEF-ROC-051/052) and a guard keyed on a plausible-but-wrong category would false-green the whole fix (DEF-AIDX-005). Jira is NEVER read back to resolve the ambiguity (EXP-047). DEF-ROC-144 ADDS THE SECOND QUESTION, which was being answered by the first: classifyCreateFailure says whether a ticket EXISTS, never whether another attempt is worth making, and callers conflated the two so EVERY refusal was rethrown as if transient. refusalPermanence splits the refused set by the REAL HTTP status (failureStatus, read off the same JiraRequestError shape as failureCategory so a log line and both decisions cannot disagree): RETRYABLE_REFUSAL_STATUSES = 408/429 only - a rate limit or a service-side timeout a retry genuinely can change - and 400/401/403/404 are PERMANENT, refused identically for ever. FAIL-SAFE DIRECTION IS THE OPPOSITE OF classifyCreateFailure ON PURPOSE: an unenumerated/absent/non-numeric status is PERMANENT, which is safe ONLY because the caller has already made the decision record durable - a wrong permanent costs a 